    {
      "id": "finite-difference-derivatives-r2-q1",
      "round": 2,
      "type": "symbolic",
      "prompt": "Let \\(a\\) and \\(h\\) be real, with \\(h\\) nonzero. The forward difference of \\(a t^2\\) at \\(t = 3\\), using step \\(h\\), is an expression in the names a and h. Write that expression.",
      "explanation": "The values are \\(9a\\) and \\(a(3 + h)^2\\). Their difference, divided by \\(h\\), simplifies to \\(a(6 + h)\\).\n• \\(6a\\) is the exact derivative, missing the truncation \\(a h\\).\n• \\(a(3 + h)^2\\) is the shifted function value, not a quotient.\n• \\(6a + h\\) drops the factor \\(a\\) on the truncation term.",
